viewWithTag: searches front to back, which is what decides a tie.

-[NSView viewWithTag:] walked the subview array forwards. For a tag that appears once, the
direction cannot be observed at all: forwards and backwards return the same view. It decides
exactly one thing, which of several siblings sharing a tag is returned, and that is the case
MoneyMoney has.

MoneyMoney lost its entire Preferences window to it. showPreferences: does

    id v = [[[self popoverController] view] viewWithTag: 1];
    [v setTitle: ...];
    [v setHasPriority: YES];

and the popover it searches is PreferencesWindow.nib obj[91], an NSCustomView whose subviews in
ARCHIVE ORDER begin

    [ 0] obj[ 93] MMHelpButton NSTag=1
    [ 1] obj[ 83] MMButton     NSTag=2
    [ 2] obj[ 97] MMButton     NSTag=1

Searching forwards hands the caller the help button at index 0. setHasPriority: is MMButton's, so
it raises, cocotron catches it in the @try around action delivery, and the rest of showPreferences:
never runs: no ordering call, no surface, and ZERO occurrences of the word Exception in the log.
Searching front to back returns obj[97], the price button, which is the only view tagged 1 in the
whole bundle that is an MMButton.

EVERYTHING ELSE WAS EXONERATED FIRST, by measurement rather than by reasoning. With
CIDER_TRACE_VIEWTAG the walk visits the tags the archive says it should, in the order the archive
says, for both roots the application searches, so the nib decode, the class swapping, the tag
values and the subview order are all faithful. probe_dynamic_method showed the runtime consults
resolveInstanceMethod:, forwardingTargetForSelector: and forwardInvocation:, so it is not a
dispatch hook. Nothing removes the help button in our run: 47 removeFromSuperview calls, 0 on
MMHelpButton. What was left was the direction of the search.

THE APPLICATION IS THE ARBITER and the direction is not documented. With this change Preferences
opens, renders its toolbar and General pane, and a click on Security switches to that pane. That is
the evidence; the ordering is a deliberate choice to match the shipping application rather than a
fact read out of a header.

THE BLAST RADIUS IS MEASURED, not assumed. Across 41 nibs in three roster applications there are
293 parents with subviews and exactly 3 hold a duplicate tag among their children, all in
MoneyMoney. Every other search in the roster returns the same view either way.

The trace gained the receiver pointer, the superview class and the window, because two searches ran
back to back and only the identity separated which one fed the raise.

Task #254.

